= Dataset Analysis

#v(1em)

The dataset we are using for this project is the #link("https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/gpiosenka/sports-classification")[100 Sports Image Classification] dataset. It includes 14492 images, divided into 100 different classes. The images are all 224x224 pixels, and they all use JPG format. The classes are not balanced, with a range in between 69 and 201 images.

[Put class distribution image here]

The dataset is distributed by default in three folders:
- train/ has 13493 files
- test/ has 500 files
- valid/ has 500 files
There is also a CSV file with basic information:

[Put head() of dataframe]

And additionally there is HDF5 file ('EfficientNetB0-100-(224 X 224)- 98.40.h5') that is not mentioned in the dataset documentation, but is asumed to be a pre-trained model that can be used for tasks such as transfer learning or quick predictions.

The author of the dataset claims that "This dataset was carefully created so that the region of interest (ROI) in this case the sport occupies approximately 50% of the pixels in the image. As a consequence even models of moderate complexity should achieve training and validation accuracies in the high 90's."

Due to the amount of classes, we decided to remove some of them in order to carry out our analysis. We decided to test some different combinations:
- 3 very different classes [which ones]
- 3 similar classes [which ones]
- 6 classes [which ones]
- 100 classes??

Class choice for each experiment was based on class distribution, prioriticing classese with higher amount of images.

